<Layout title="How to Choose Lighting for a New Construction Project | ATECT Architecture" description="Learn how to approach lighting for a new construction project by considering function, atmosphere, daylight, controls, and architectural design.">
  <article class="article"><header><p class="eyebrow">Home & Interiors</p><h1>How to Choose Lighting for a New Construction Project</h1><p class="lede">Lighting works best when it is planned with the architecture. Start with how each space will be used, then layer ambient, task, accent, and decorative light where appropriate.</p></header>
  <section><h2>Start with function</h2><p>Identify the activities that happen in each room. Kitchens, work areas, circulation spaces, bathrooms, bedrooms, and entertaining areas can require different lighting approaches.</p></section>
  <section><h2>Use daylight as part of the design</h2><p>Window placement, orientation, shading, and room depth affect daylight. Electric lighting should complement those conditions rather than treating every room the same.</p></section>
  <section><h2>Think in layers</h2><p>Ambient lighting provides general illumination, task lighting supports specific activities, and accent or decorative lighting can add emphasis and atmosphere.</p></section>
  <section><h2>Coordinate early</h2><p>Fixture locations, ceiling conditions, switches, controls, cabinetry, mechanical systems, and structural elements can affect one another. Coordinating these items during design can reduce conflicts later.</p></section>
  <section class="callout"><h2>Designing a new home or renovation?</h2><p>ATECT Architecture can coordinate lighting and interior decisions as part of a larger residential design and documentation process.</p><a class="button dark" href="/contact">Start a Project</a></section>
  </article>
</Layout>
